<handout>
<title>Adding and Subtraction Rational Expressions</title>
<page>
    <objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section we will see how to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Add and subtract rational expressions with a common denominator.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Find a common denominator.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Add and subtract rational expressions with different denominators.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
    </objectives>
    <p>
        Recall, that to be able to add or subtract fractions, the fractions need the same, common denominator. The same is true for rational expressions.
    </p>
    <example workspace="2.5in">
    <statement>
        <p>
          Add: <m> \frac{8x-9}{7} + \frac{10x-8}{7}</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>\frac{18x-17}{7}</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Since there is a common denominator, add the like terms in the denominator. 
        <md>
        \frac{8x-9}{7} + \frac{10x-8}{7} = \frac{8x+10x-9-8}{7} = \frac{18x-17}{7}
        </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
    </example>
    <p>
        Note that for subtracting, you need to distribute the minus sign through the numerator of the second fraction.
    </p>
    <example workspace="2.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Subtract: <m>\frac{5x+5}{10} - \frac{8x-3}{10}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{-3x+8}{10}</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
            Since there is a common denominator, we can combine the like terms in the numerator, remembering to distribute a minus sign through the second set of terms.
            <md>
                \frac{5x+5}{10} - \frac{8x-3}{10} = \frac{5x+5-(8x-3)}{10} = \frac{5x+5-8x+3}{10} = \frac{-3x+8}{10}
            </md>
            </p>
            </solution>
    </example>
</page>
<page>
    <p>
     Once, we have added or subtracted rational expressions, the result may sometimes also be simplified. We have seen this before. Factor the numerator and denominator and 
    cancel out common factors, remembering to say where the denominator is zero.   
    </p>
    <example workspace="3.0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Add: <m>\frac{x^2+5x}{x^2+2x}+\frac{x^2-4x}{x^2+2x}</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
     <answer>
        <p>
            <m>\frac{2x+1}{x+2}</m>, <m>x \ne 0</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Start by combining like terms in the numerator:
            <md>
                \frac{x^2+5x}{x^2+2x}+\frac{x^2-4x}{x^2+2x} = \frac{x^2+5x+x^2-4x}{x^2+2x} = \frac{2x^2+x}{x^2+2x}
            </md>
            Now factor the numerator and denominator and cancel:
            <md>
                \frac{2x^2+x}{x^2+2x} = \frac{x(2x+1)}{x(x+2)} = \frac{2x+1}{x+2}
            </md>
            And don't forget to note that <m>x\ne 0</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<example workspace="3.0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Add: <m>\frac{x^2-9x}{x^2+2x-48}-\frac{36-9x}{x^2+2x-48}</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
     <answer>
        <p>
            <m>\frac{x+6}{x+8}</m>, <m>x \ne 6</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Start by combining like terms in the numerator:
            <md>
                \frac{x^2-9x}{x^2+2x-48}-\frac{36-9x}{x^2+2x-48} = \frac{x^2-9x-(36-9x)}{x^2+2x-48} = \frac{x^2+9x-9x-36}{x^2+2x-48} = \frac{x^2-36}{x^2+2x-48}
            </md>
            Now factor the numerator and denominator and cancel:
            <md>
                \frac{x^2-36}{x^2+2x-48} = \frac{(x+6)(x-6)}{(x-6)(x+8)} = \frac{x+6}{x+8}
            </md>
            And don't forget to note that <m>x\ne 6</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<p>
    So far, we have only seen fractions with common denominators. What if we start with rational expressions with different denominators? We need to start by finding
    a common denominator. Recall from adding and subtracting numerical fractions, you can find a common denominator by first factoring each denominator to see if there are
    any common factors and then adding factors to each fraction until the denominators match.
</p>
<example workspace="2.0in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Find the common denominator of <m>\frac{6x}{x^2-81} + \frac{x}{x-9}</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>(x-9)(x+9)</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Factor each denominator to get:
            <md>
                \frac{6x}{(x+9)(x-9)} + \frac{x}{x-9}
            </md>
            Both denominators include <m>(x-9)</m>. The first fraction also has a <m>(x+9)</m> factor. We need to add this the second fraction so that the fractions now
            have a common denominator. When we add a new factor to a denominator, we also need to add this factor to the numerator of that fraction.
            <md>
                \frac{6x}{(x+9)(x-9)} + \frac{x(x+9)}{(x-9)(x+9)}
            </md>
            Leave the denominator factored! Remember we need the denominator factored to further simplify it later.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
</page>
<page>
    <p>
        Once we have a common denominator, we can proceed with adding or subtracting...
    </p>
</page>
</handout>
